 "standard_text": "Definition of a polynomial in one variable, coefficients, terms and zero polynomial; degree; constant, linear, quadratic and cubic polynomials; monomials, binomials, trinomials; factors and multiples; zeros of a polynomial; the Factor Theorem; factorisation of ax² + bx + c and of cubic polynomials using the Factor Theorem; algebraic identities including (x + y + z)², (x ± y)³ and x³ + y³ + z³ − 3xyz (summary of the CBSE syllabus)",
 "meta_desc": "Class 9 Polynomials worksheet (NCERT Chapter 2): degree and coefficients, values and zeroes, Factor Theorem, factorisation and algebraic identities. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "p(a) = 0 ⇔ (x − a) is a factor",
 "key_sub": "Factor Theorem: test a value to find a factor.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>A <b>polynomial</b> in x has only whole-number powers of x. Its <b>degree</b> is the highest power. A <b>zero</b> of p(x) is a value a with p(a) = 0.</p><p><b>Factor Theorem</b>: (x − a) is a factor of p(x) exactly when p(a) = 0.</p><p>Identities: (x + y)² = x² + 2xy + y², x² − y² = (x + y)(x − y), (x + y)³ = x³ + y³ + 3xy(x + y), x³ + y³ + z³ − 3xyz = (x + y + z)(x² + y² + z² − xy − yz − zx).</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Is (x + 1) a factor of x³ + x² + x + 1?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>p(−1) = −1 + 1 − 1 + 1 = 0</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: Yes</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Factorise 6x² + 17x + 5.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>6 × 5 = 30 = 15 × 2, 15 + 2 = 17</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>6x² + 15x + 2x + 5 = 3x(2x + 5) + (2x + 5)</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: (3x + 1)(2x + 5)</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Finding the degree by counting terms. Use the highest power.</li><li>Testing p(a) for the factor (x + a). For x + a, test p(−a).</li><li>Writing (x + y)³ = x³ + y³. The middle terms 3x²y + 3xy² are missing.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the Factor Theorem?",
   "a": "If p(a) = 0 then (x − a) is a factor of p(x), and if (x − a) is a factor then p(a) = 0."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I factorise ax² + bx + c by splitting the middle term?",
   "a": "Find two numbers whose product is ac and whose sum is b, rewrite bx as the sum of two terms, then factorise in pairs."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is the zero of a linear polynomial ax + b?",
   "a": "x = −b/a."
  },
  {
   "q": "If a + b + c = 0, what is a³ + b³ + c³?",
   "a": "3abc, from the identity a³ + b³ + c³ − 3abc = (a + b + c)(a² + b² + c² − ab − bc − ca)."
  }
